Historically, these domains have been addressed via fragmented codes. However, under extreme dynamic stress (near-horizon collapse or super-critical ignition), modular approximations break down due to boundary feedback latency and numerical stiffness. The \textbf{Sovereign Simulation Architecture} unifies these disparate physical operators into a single, cohesive tensor-processing pipeline.

\section{The $3+1$ ADM Spacetime Split}
We formulate the spacetime geometry on a pseudo-Riemannian manifold $(M, g_{\mu\nu})$ governed by the Einstein field equations. Employing the Arnowitt-Deser-Misner (ADM) $3+1$ foliation, the spacetime line element is expressed as:
\begin{equation}
ds^2 = -\alpha^2 dt^2 + \gamma_{ij} (dx^i + \beta^i dt)(dx^j + \beta^j dt)
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ is the lapse function, $\beta^i$ is the spatial shift vector, and $\gamma_{ij}$ represents the three-dimensional spatial metric tensor induced on hyperspatial slices, with determinant $\gamma = \det(\gamma_{ij})$.

\section{Relativistic Conservation Laws}
The macroscopic behavior of the magnetized, conducting fluid is governed by local conservation of baryon number and the divergence-free nature of the total stress-energy tensor $T^{\mu\nu}$:
\begin{align}
\nabla_\mu (\rho u^\mu) &= 0 \\
\nabla_\nu T^{\mu\nu} &= 0
\end{align}
where $\rho$ is the rest-mass density and $u^\mu$ is the fluid four-velocity, normalized such that $u^\mu u_\mu = -1$.

