\section{The Valencia Formulation}
Assuming infinite electrical conductivity (ideal MHD), the total stress-energy tensor combines fluid enthalpy and electromagnetic field contributions:
\begin{equation}
T^{\mu\nu} = \left( \rho h + b^2 \right) u^\mu u^\nu + \left( P + \frac{1}{2}b^2 \right) g^{\mu\nu} - b^\mu b^\nu
\end{equation}
where $h = 1 + \epsilon + \frac{P}{\rho}$ is the specific enthalpy, with specific internal energy $\epsilon$ and isotropic thermal pressure $P$. Primitive variables $\mathbf{w} = (\rho, v^i, P, B^i)$ are mapped into the Valencia conservative vector $\mathbf{U}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{U} = \sqrt{-g} \begin{pmatrix} D \\ S_j \\ \tau \\ B^k \end{pmatrix} = \sqrt{-g} \begin{pmatrix} \rho W \\ \rho h W^2 v_j \\ \rho h W^2 - P - \rho W + \frac{1}{2} b^2 \\ B^k \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
where $W = \alpha u^t = (1 - v_i v^i)^{-1/2}$ is the Eulerian Lorentz factor.

\section{Harten-Lax-van Leer Approximate Riemann Solvers}
